      {
        "id": "constant-push-means-constant-speed",
        "statement": "A steady push should make a steady speed.",
        "remediation": "A steady push that does not match another force keeps adding speed. The speed only holds when the net force is zero."
      }
    ],
    "debriefExplanation": "The first run had two pushes of the same size, one each way. They cancel, so the net force was 0 N. The cart kept the same speed the whole time. The second run had one push of 4 N and one of 1 N. The net force was 3 N, so the cart built up speed. Equal and opposite forces do not stop a cart. They only stop it from changing speed.",
